diff --git a/docs/specs/virt-smp.rst b/docs/specs/virt-smp.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..cdc596d5e7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/specs/virt-smp.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Virtual SMP Controller
+======================
+
+The ``virt-smp`` device provides multiprocessor support for the m68k
+``virt`` machine, which has no hardware reference implementation. It is
+instantiated only when the machine has more than one CPU (``-smp N``,
+N > 1, at most 16). With a single CPU the machine is unchanged.
+
+It provides CPU identification, secondary CPU start and stop,
+inter-processor interrupts (IPIs), a timer per CPU, a global nanosecond
+counter, and per-CPU interrupt delivery, including the routing of each
+goldfish-pic output level to a selected CPU. It replaces the
+``m68k-irq-controller`` of the single CPU machine.
+
+Discovery
+---------
+
+The ``BI_VIRT_SMP_BASE`` (0x8007) bootinfo record contains the MMIO base
+address of the device and the number of CPUs.
+
+Programming model
+-----------------
+
+The device uses 4 KiB of MMIO space. All registers are 32-bit wide, big
+endian, and must be accessed with aligned 32-bit accesses. CPUs are
+numbered 0 to N-1; CPU 0 is the boot CPU.
+
+Registers in the banked window (0x000-0x0ff) belong to the CPU that does
+the access.
+
+====== ============== ====== ==================================================
+Offset Name           Access Description
+====== ============== ====== ==================================================
+0x000  ID             RO     Number of the accessing CPU
+0x004  NCPUS          RO     Number of CPUs
+0x008  VERSION        RO     1
+0x010  IPI_PENDING    RO     Pending IPI reasons (bits 15..0)
+0x014  IPI_CLEAR      W1C    Clear the IPI reasons written as 1
+0x018  IPI_CONFIG     RW     Bits 2..0: IPL (0: disabled), bits 15..8: vector.
+                             Reset value: IPL 6, vector 0x40
+0x020  TIMER_CTRL     RW     Bit 0: enable, bit 1: periodic. A write
+                             (re)starts the countdown from TIMER_INTERVAL
+0x024  TIMER_INTERVAL RW     Countdown in nanoseconds
+0x028  TIMER_STATUS   RW     Bit 0: expired; write 1 to acknowledge
+0x02c  TIMER_CONFIG   RW     Bits 2..0: IPL (0: disabled), bits 15..8: vector.
+                             Reset value: IPL 6, vector 0x41
+====== ============== ====== ==================================================
+
+Global registers:
+
+=========== ============== ====== =============================================
+Offset      Name           Access Description
+=========== ============== ====== =============================================
+0x100       IPI_SEND       WO     Bits 31..16: reasons, bits 15..0: target CPU
+                                  mask. The reasons are ORed into IPI_PENDING
+                                  of each running target CPU
+0x110       DEVIRQ_ROUTE   RW     4 bits per level L (1..7) at bit 4*(L-1):
+                                  the CPU that receives goldfish-pic #L
+                                  (L=1..6) and NMI (L=7). Reset value: 0
+0x120       COUNTER_LO     RO     Nanosecond counter (QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL),
+                                  low 32 bits; latches COUNTER_HI for the
+                                  accessing CPU
+0x124       COUNTER_HI     RO     High 32 bits latched by COUNTER_LO
+0x200+16*n  START_PC(n)    RW     Initial PC of CPU n
+0x204+16*n  START_SP(n)    RW     Initial A7 (interrupt stack) of CPU n
+0x208+16*n  START_ARG(n)   RW     Initial D0 and A0 of CPU n
+0x20c+16*n  CPU_CTRL(n)    RW     Read: 1 if running. Write 1: start CPU n if
+                                  stopped. Write 0: stop CPU n (ignored for
+                                  CPU 0)
+=========== ============== ====== =============================================
+
+A started CPU begins in its reset state (supervisor mode, IPL 7, MMU off,
+VBR 0) with the PC, A7, D0 and A0 given above. Its per-CPU registers are
+reset when it is started or stopped. A stopped CPU does not execute and
+takes no interrupt; IPIs sent to it are discarded. Starting a CPU is
+asynchronous: the guest must use its own handshake to know when the new
+CPU runs. A machine reset stops all CPUs except CPU 0.
+
+Interrupt delivery
+------------------
+
+Each CPU is interrupted at the highest level among: the goldfish-pic
+outputs routed to it (autovectored), its IPI and its timer (using the
+vectors of IPI_CONFIG and TIMER_CONFIG). At equal levels the IPI has the
+highest priority, then the timer, then the goldfish-pic. All sources are
+level triggered and stay asserted until acknowledged.
